<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-documents.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-documents-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-documents.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim glossary</span></nav><p class="tag">Reference</p><h1>Property Insurance Claim Glossary</h1><p class="lede">47 terms from policies, estimates and appraisal awards — including island-specific ones like AOAO, GET, HPIA and lava zone — in plain English.</p></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “umpire”, “vog” or “depreciation”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gcount></p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-G">G</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-K">K</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-V">V</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and release the rest after repairs are complete.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expenses (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the increase in living costs, such as rent or meals, while your home is uninhabitable after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It is the question an appraisal panel normally decides.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>AOAO</dt><dd>Association of Apartment Owners – the governing body of a Hawaii condominium, which typically buys the master policy for the building.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision, signed by at least two of the three panel members, that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision allowing either party to demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side chooses to value the loss in appraisal. Policies usually require the appraiser to be competent and disinterested.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-B"><dt>Betterment</dt><dd>An improvement beyond the property’s pre-loss condition. Insurers may reduce payment for betterment, depending on policy wording.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-C"><dt>Coverage A–D</dt><dd>The main parts of a homeowners policy: A is the dwelling, B other structures, C personal property and D loss of use.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-D"><dt>Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for hauling away damaged materials after a covered loss, sometimes subject to a separate limit. Island disposal and freight costs can make it significant.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, property, coverage limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss you pay before the insurer pays. Hawaii policies may have separate deductibles for hurricane or other perils.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition, used to calculate actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-E"><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Estimate</dt><dd>An itemized calculation of the cost to repair or replace damaged property, typically broken into line items.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-G"><dt>General excise tax (GET)</dt><dd>Hawaii’s tax on business gross income, including contractor work, which is commonly passed on to customers. Rates vary by county, roughly 4% to about 4.7%.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-H"><dt>Hawaii Hurricane Relief Fund (HHRF)</dt><dd>A state fund created after Hurricane Iniki to provide hurricane coverage when private carriers pulled back. It later stopped writing policies once private coverage returned.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>HO-6</dt><dd>A condominium unit-owner policy covering the unit’s interior, contents, loss of use and often loss assessments.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>HPIA</dt><dd>The Hawaii Property Insurance Association, the state’s FAIR plan, best known for basic property coverage in Big Island lava zones 1 and 2.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Hurricane deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible, often a percentage of dwelling coverage, that applies when a hurricane causes the loss as defined in the policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-I"><dt>Insurance Division (DCCA)</dt><dd>The Hawaii state regulator of insurers, part of the Department of Commerce and Consumer Affairs and headed by the Insurance Commissioner. It accepts consumer complaints and inquiries.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-K"><dt>Kona low</dt><dd>A winter storm system that brings heavy rain and southerly winds from the usually sheltered leeward side of the islands.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-L"><dt>Lava zone</dt><dd>One of nine USGS lava hazard zones on the Big Island, with zone 1 the most hazardous. Zone affects insurance availability and pricing.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate describing one task or material, with a quantity, unit and price.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Loss assessment coverage</dt><dd>HO-6 coverage for your share of a special assessment the AOAO levies after a covered loss, up to the limit stated.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-M"><dt>Master policy</dt><dd>The policy an AOAO buys to insure the building and common elements of a condominium.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match in color, texture or style. Treatment depends on policy wording and law.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out water. Most policies require it.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-N"><dt>Named peril</dt><dd>A policy that covers only the causes of loss specifically listed, such as fire or windstorm.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-O"><dt>Open peril</dt><dd>A policy that covers all causes of loss except those specifically excluded.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to bring repaired property up to current building codes, often subject to a separate limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>The general contractor’s charges for supervision, coordination and profit, often figured as a percentage of the job.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which some policies require within a set time or on request.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional who represents policyholders in preparing and negotiating a claim, usually for a percentage fee.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation withheld from an initial payment that the insurer pays once repairs are completed, under replacement cost policies.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which an insurer handles a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later. It is a signal to consult an attorney.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of what was damaged and what work is needed to repair it, before pricing.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Subrogation</dt><dd>The insurer’s right, after paying a claim, to recover from a third party who caused the loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy provision setting how long you have to file a lawsuit against the insurer. Appraisal does not necessarily pause it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for damage or costs not included in the original estimate, often found during repairs.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-T"><dt>Total loss</dt><dd>A loss where the property is destroyed or repairs would cost more than the policy limit or the property’s value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Trade winds</dt><dd>The steady northeasterly winds that dominate Hawaii’s weather, keeping windward slopes wet and leeward areas dry.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-V"><dt>Vog</dt><dd>Volcanic smog formed from volcanic gases, most common on the Big Island’s west side. It can contribute to corrosion over time.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers, adjusters and contractors to price repairs by line item and region.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="aside"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="big" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn sun" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 7am–5pm HST</small></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/appraisal-process-map/">Appraisal process map</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-questions-by-topic/">Questions by topic</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-glossary/" aria-current="page">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/">Hawaii claim guides</a></li></ul></div></aside></div>
</main>
